{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Come to Pharaoh.</b> This principle is in your hand — every place it says ״בֹּא אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ the intent is to tell him that he should enter his inner chamber without asking permission, even though the kings' custom was to have guards of the house; even so he would go in and enter before him without asking permission. And so he did, as they said of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 175) that Pharaoh had many guards armed with swords and lions and dogs, and he [Moses] would enter with none to stop him. And I found clear proof for their words of blessed memory from what Pharaoh said to Moses (Exodus 10:28): ״הִשָּׁמֶר לְךָ וְגוֹ׳ אַל תּוֹסֶף רְאוֹת פָּנַי״ and so forth — why did Pharaoh need to warn Moses? He should have commanded his servants the guards of the house, for such is the custom of kings to station guards of the king and to them he would give orders not to let him enter. Rather it is certainly so that they did not stand against him — not even lions — and this was a great miracle. Therefore HaShem needed to say to him: ״בֹּא אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ — meaning: enter and go up to him and do not set your heart on any preventer in the world. And at the time when Pharaoh was going out to the water, He would say to him ״לֵךְ אֶל פַּרְעֹה״, for there there are no guards to fear, such that He would need to say to him ״עֲלֵה אֵלָיו״; but rather because that time is not fit for going, since he was relieving himself there and it is not proper conduct, therefore He says to him ״לֵךְ״ even so."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "Reading בּוֹלְעוֹ הַתָּעוּב as a corruption or dialectal form of בֵּית הַכִּסֵּא (relieving himself); translated hyper-literally as 'relieving himself there' to convey the sense of the context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:02.447374880Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}